                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "\"Playing [i]Yonder: The Cloud Catcher Chronicles[/i] was frequently frustrating because I couldn’t figure out whether the game was flawed or not right for me at this time. However, after bouncing some of my gripes off friends and finishing the main story, I think I can safely say that it’s not me — it’s the game. There are glimmers of a good time here, and I’d love to see the developers attempt to refine things for a sequel. In fact, as I write this, I see that they put out another game a few years ago that looks like a spiritual successor of sorts to this one, so maybe I’ll have to check that out at some point to see if it improves upon some of my gripes. As for [i]Yonder[/i], I'll give credit where it's due: the world is charming, with cute critters and lovely landscapes making it cozy to just exist in. However, as soon as all the warm, fluffy stuff grinds up against the cold, hard systems at play, that’s when the problems start to emerge. Trading is a finicky chore, and crafting is worse. The only real incentive for doing a lot of things is “do it because you want to”, which should be freeing, but instead makes those systems feel tacked-on and the game as a whole feel aimless. And there are enough things that simply feel like busywork to where even the mere 9 hours I spent with Yonder had times where I was saying, “Is this really all there is?” It may look inviting, but [i]Yonder: The Cloud Catcher Chronicles[/i] is one journey I think you’re safe to skip.\"\r\n\r\nFull review [url=https://olivigarden.ca/2026/10/23/yonder-the-cloud-catcher-chronicles-review/]here[/url]",
